WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.260 --> 00:00:01.630
Welcome to mCoding.

00:00:01.630 --> 00:00:05.020
Today we're hitting a common problem with
some recursive algorithms causing them to

00:00:05.020 --> 00:00:08.610
fail on even moderately sized inputs.

00:00:08.610 --> 00:00:11.509
Let's say you have to traverse a tree in some
way.

00:00:11.509 --> 00:00:17.480
The tree is made up of nodes that contain
some data and zero or more child nodes.

00:00:17.480 --> 00:00:21.770
Let's take a dead simple recursive task, just
printing out the nodes.

00:00:21.770 --> 00:00:25.859
Here's the whole implementation, yes it's
that simple.

00:00:25.859 --> 00:00:31.980
We print out the current node, then loop over
the children, recursing on each child.

00:00:31.980 --> 00:00:36.100
The order we are going in here is called a
pre-order traversal of the tree, by the way.

00:00:36.100 --> 00:00:39.800
And it works!

00:00:39.800 --> 00:00:44.550
When we give it this small example, it prints
out all the nodes in the expected order.

00:00:44.550 --> 00:00:52.480
First the root, then child 0, the three children
of child 0 which are 0-0, 0-1, and 0-2, and

00:00:52.480 --> 00:00:55.440
then child 1 of the root.

00:00:55.440 --> 00:00:59.910
So great, all your tests pass, it's simple
and readable, you ship it to production

00:00:59.910 --> 00:01:06.240
… and the second a client touches it, whamo
bamo it instantly breaks.

00:01:06.240 --> 00:01:11.320
Surely they must have passed a ginormous,
unsightly, pathological tree in order to break

00:01:11.320 --> 00:01:16.900
the perfection and elegance that is every
line of code beget of your golden fingertips?

00:01:16.900 --> 00:01:23.510
Uhh nope, they passed a linked list with 1000
nodes in it.

00:01:23.510 --> 00:01:29.950
What went wrong is you hit the recursion limit,
which is a mere 1000 in Python by default.

00:01:29.950 --> 00:01:33.299
That's why the crash happens near 1000 children.

00:01:33.299 --> 00:01:37.630
You can increase the recursion limit using
sys.setrecursionlimit but that doesn't really

00:01:37.630 --> 00:01:39.770
solve the problem.

00:01:39.770 --> 00:01:43.969
The recursion limit is there to fail early
in the case when you accidentally cause an

00:01:43.969 --> 00:01:48.049
infinite recursion, so you can crash the program
instead of running out of memory and crashing

00:01:48.049 --> 00:01:50.050
the whole computer.

00:01:50.050 --> 00:01:53.409
Increasing the limit a lot would defeat the
purpose of having the limit.

00:01:53.409 --> 00:01:58.660
But only increasing it a little bit is just
asking for the client to hand you a 2000 node

00:01:58.660 --> 00:02:00.900
list in the future.

00:02:00.900 --> 00:02:01.900
So what do we do?

00:02:01.900 --> 00:02:04.799
We use this handy trick.

00:02:04.799 --> 00:02:09.590
The idea is to convert your recursive call
stack into a literal call stack, making the

00:02:09.590 --> 00:02:15.640
entire function iterative, thereby completely
bypassing any recursion limit problems.

00:02:15.640 --> 00:02:19.010
We'll use a list as the data structure for
our stack.

00:02:19.010 --> 00:02:23.090
Append is pushing onto the stack and pop is
popping off the stack.

00:02:23.090 --> 00:02:29.170
We'll use a "while stack" to keep going as
long as our stack is not empty.

00:02:29.170 --> 00:02:34.640
Then we indent this, and replace any recursive
calls like this one with pushing onto the

00:02:34.640 --> 00:02:36.480
stack.

00:02:36.480 --> 00:02:39.780
Running our linked list example again, we
see now it fully prints all the children down

00:02:39.780 --> 00:02:43.530
to child 999.

00:02:43.530 --> 00:02:46.890
But if we take a look at our first smaller
example, we'll see that we've actually changed

00:02:46.890 --> 00:02:48.390
the order that nodes are printing in.

00:02:48.390 --> 00:02:58.290
Before we had the root, then child 0, then
0-0, 0-1, and 0-2, finishing with child 1

00:02:58.290 --> 00:02:59.290
last.

00:02:59.290 --> 00:03:04.140
And the reason for that is because a stack
is a last-in-first-out data structure.

00:03:04.140 --> 00:03:08.260
The fix for this is that in our implementation,
whenever we want to make multiple recursive

00:03:08.260 --> 00:03:11.950
calls, we actually need to put them on the
stack in reverse order, so they go in the

00:03:11.950 --> 00:03:15.750
intended order when we pop them off the stack.

00:03:15.750 --> 00:03:19.780
So we just loop over each node's children
in reverse order.

00:03:19.780 --> 00:03:22.420
Then we're back to the same output as before.

00:03:22.420 --> 00:03:26.390
We now have an equivalent function to the
original recursive implementation that prints

00:03:26.390 --> 00:03:30.980
our nodes and that works no matter how deep
a tree you pass it.

00:03:30.980 --> 00:03:34.510
But you may be wondering, what if I had a
more complex recursive function that doesn't

00:03:34.510 --> 00:03:38.220
just have a single node parameter like this
one?

00:03:38.220 --> 00:03:41.950
Let's say I wanted to add a max depth to my
print.

00:03:41.950 --> 00:03:46.739
In the recursive case, that means checking
if we've hit the max depth and not recursing

00:03:46.739 --> 00:03:48.810
if we have.

00:03:48.810 --> 00:03:53.760
Otherwise we do recurse and pass each child
one less max depth.

00:03:53.760 --> 00:03:57.379
To make the iterative version, we do the same
trick as before, but instead of just putting

00:03:57.379 --> 00:04:01.310
the current node on the stack, we'll put a
tuple of whatever arguments we were going

00:04:01.310 --> 00:04:03.670
to pass to the recursive call.

00:04:03.670 --> 00:04:07.680
We start by making a stack with the initial
arguments to the function.

00:04:07.680 --> 00:04:12.829
Then we do a "while stack", pop off an element
from the stack, and unpack the variables.

00:04:12.829 --> 00:04:16.310
Popping the tuple off the stack and assigning
to these variables restores all of them to

00:04:16.310 --> 00:04:20.340
a state just like we would have had if we
made a recursive call.

00:04:20.340 --> 00:04:22.770
Once again we indent.

00:04:22.770 --> 00:04:26.400
An early return in the recursive implementation
just means that we don't want to do any more

00:04:26.400 --> 00:04:29.000
work in this iteration.

00:04:29.000 --> 00:04:33.120
So in the stack version, that just becomes
a continue, which will skip and process the

00:04:33.120 --> 00:04:34.889
next item on the stack.

00:04:34.889 --> 00:04:38.690
Then we replace recursive calls with just
pushing onto the stack any arguments that

00:04:38.690 --> 00:04:41.560
we were going to pass to the recursive call.

00:04:41.560 --> 00:04:46.130
And as before, we need to put those things
on the stack in the reverse order.

00:04:46.130 --> 00:04:49.460
And now our max depth parameter is working.

00:04:49.460 --> 00:04:51.520
Max depth of 0 just means printing the root.

00:04:51.520 --> 00:04:55.770
If we have a depth of 1 then we'll use the
root and its children, but we don't recurse

00:04:55.770 --> 00:04:58.139
to their children.

00:04:58.139 --> 00:05:02.500
And a max depth of 2 or higher for this tree
will print the entire thing.

00:05:02.500 --> 00:05:05.990
So that's the trick, it's just an easy way
to convert any recursive function into an

00:05:05.990 --> 00:05:08.310
iterative one.

00:05:08.310 --> 00:05:13.000
But I did want to finish by just pointing
out one other issue with this code.

00:05:13.000 --> 00:05:18.900
Both the recursive and iterative solutions
still suffer from high coupling.

00:05:18.900 --> 00:05:23.729
Namely, this function is actually doing two
things.

00:05:23.729 --> 00:05:27.090
Number 1, it's iterating over the tree of
nodes.

00:05:27.090 --> 00:05:32.430
And number 2, it's also operating on the data,
in this case printing out the nodes.

00:05:32.430 --> 00:05:36.910
If we wanted to do something else besides
just print the node, we may end up copy-pasting

00:05:36.910 --> 00:05:40.770
this entire block of code, only to change
one line.

00:05:40.770 --> 00:05:46.759
As you can imagine, copy-pasting huge blocks
of code just to change one line is discourages.

00:05:46.759 --> 00:05:51.030
Instead let's decouple these things by pulling
out the part that does the iteration and making

00:05:51.030 --> 00:05:53.020
it a generator.

00:05:53.020 --> 00:05:57.039
Instead of actually doing anything with the
node, let's just yield the node so that someone

00:05:57.039 --> 00:05:59.730
else can do something with it.

00:05:59.730 --> 00:06:01.910
Change the name of course.

00:06:01.910 --> 00:06:07.050
Then we can implement our printing function
just by iterating over the nodes and printing.

00:06:07.050 --> 00:06:12.030
Now neither the printing code nor the iteration
code needs to know anything about each other,

00:06:12.030 --> 00:06:16.370
meaning they are completely decoupled, allowing
you to plug in a different operation or different

00:06:16.370 --> 00:06:20.270
order of iteration without rewriting or duplicating
code.

00:06:20.270 --> 00:06:23.580
As far as functionality goes of course it's
the same as it was before.

00:06:23.580 --> 00:06:26.960
But especially in larger projects, this is
going to make your code much more testable

00:06:26.960 --> 00:06:29.540
and maintainable for the future.

00:06:29.540 --> 00:06:31.900
So just keep that in mind.

00:06:31.900 --> 00:06:34.560
Anyway I hope you enjoyed the trick and this
video.

00:06:34.560 --> 00:06:37.900
If you did don't forget to subscribe and if
you especially like the video please consider

00:06:37.900 --> 00:06:41.970
becoming a patron on Patreon
So thank you so much and I'll see ya next

00:06:41.970 --> 00:06:42.260
time.

